# Brewfile entries an employer-managed Mac (DOTFILES_PROFILE=work in
# ~/.config/dotfiles/env) doesn't install. A separate file because
# script/update-brewfile regenerates the Brewfile with `brew bundle dump`,
# which would erase any conditionals written into it. See lib/profile.
# Format: <type> <name>, where type is brew, cask, mas or tap, or mise for a
# tool in .config/mise/config.toml. `<type> *`
# skips every entry of that type, and `!<type> <name>` opts one back in (a mas
# entry's name is its id).

# Casks and App Store apps are opt-in on a work Mac: it starts with a minimal
# app set and grows as needed. Add a `!cask` line to install one there. App
# Store apps also need a personal Apple ID, which a managed Mac may not allow,
# and a failed mas install stops brew bundle partway.
cask *
mas *

!cask font-hack-nerd-font
!cask ghostty
!cask visual-studio-code

# Desk hardware: docks, lights and mice that move between Macs
!cask displaylink
!cask elgato-control-center
!cask openlogi

# Already on the work Mac, installed by hand; adopt it once with
# `brew install --cask --adopt spotify` so brew bundle doesn't trip over it
!cask spotify

# Formulae are still installed unless listed below. The casks below are
# skipped by `cask *` anyway; they stay as the reasons never to opt them in,
# and a test rejects opting in to one of them.

# MuteDeck runs a local API server for its integrations, and listening
# ports on a corporate network draw IT's scanners.
cask mutedeck

# Network filters: AdGuard's DNS proxy and content filter fight a corporate
# VPN or secure web gateway for the same traffic. LuLu's outbound firewall is
# another network extension in the same path, and it would prompt for every
# agent IT's MDM installs.
cask adguard
cask lulu

# Security tools that overlap the employer's: BlockBlock alerts on every
# persistent agent IT's MDM or EDR installs, OverSight duplicates their
# camera and mic monitoring, and mitmproxy's TLS-intercepting CA is the kind of
# thing corporate security flags.
cask blockblock
cask oversight
cask mitmproxy

# Tools that work around or reach into what IT manages: Caffeine keeps the Mac
# awake, so the MDM's idle screen lock never fires; mole (run by `up` from a
# terminal) uses sudo to tweak the system and sweeps logs and caches that IT's
# agents may rely on; Hammerspoon needs Accessibility to run arbitrary
# scripts, with no tracked config to run here.
cask caffeine
cask hammerspoon
brew mole

# Reads everything typed in every app, and nothing here points it at the local
# LanguageTool server, so work text may go to LanguageTool's cloud. Harper
# checks on-device and stays.
cask languagetool-desktop

# Network probing: a port scan or bandwidth test from a corporate Mac can
# trip intrusion alerts, and acceptable-use policies often forbid scanning.
# mtr and gping are milder, but send the same kind of probe traffic.
brew iperf
brew nmap
brew mtr
brew gping

# Data-loss prevention watches for tools that move files off the Mac: rclone
# syncs to cloud storage, and restic backs up to a remote store (script/backup
# refuses to run on a work Mac anyway). GAM administers a Google Workspace
# domain; it's only here for script/chrome-policy, which skips a work Mac.
mise rclone
brew restic
mise pipx:gam7

# Caddy runs as a Homebrew service, and nothing here configures it: a
# listening web server on a work Mac draws questions and serves nothing.
brew caddy

# The writing and PDF pipeline for personal publishing, about 2 GB with
# OCR's language packs and Java. LanguageTool's server also runs as a
# Homebrew service listening on a local port, which a work Mac doesn't need
# for Vale and Harper to lint prose in the editor.
brew languagetool
brew openjdk
brew maven
brew pandoc
brew weasyprint
brew diction
brew ocrmypdf
brew tesseract-lang
brew img2pdf
brew pdftk-java
brew ghostscript

# Media conversion. exiftool stays: the media-privacy-pass skill needs it to
# strip metadata before a screenshot goes anywhere public.
brew ffmpeg
brew imagemagick
brew vips
brew exif

# Toolchains and cloud CLIs for personal projects, several hundred MB each.
# Opt one back in (delete its line) if a work project needs it.
brew rust
brew cmake
brew protobuf
brew jsonnet
brew hivemind
brew azure-cli

# Unused on a work Mac: mas installs App Store apps, all skipped above, and
# GPG has nothing to sign, since signing uses SSH keys (and is off there).
brew mas
brew gnupg
brew pinentry-mac

# tmpreaper deletes anything in ~/Downloads older than five days, work files
# included. Its launch agent is in config.yml's launch_agents_work_disabled.
brew tmpreaper

# Docker Desktop needs a paid subscription at larger companies; colima (with
# the docker CLI) from the Brewfile covers the same workflows.
cask docker-desktop

# Paid licences that may not cover a second Mac, and reverse-engineering
# tools an acceptable-use policy may not allow
cask hopper-disassembler
cask gifox

# Local LLMs: company AI policies often restrict them, and models are large
cask ollama-app

# Personal and consumer apps
cask google-drive
cask kindle-previewer
cask signal
cask steam
cask textream
brew epubcheck
brew gmailctl

# Radio and home-automation hobby tools
brew esphome
brew hackrf
brew librtlsdr
brew soapyhackrf
